<section class="sec sec-paper">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Mission and vision</span>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Why we exist.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
        <p class="lede">Walk alongside women recovering from drug and alcohol addiction in a Christ-centered environment as they journey toward self sufficiency.</p>
        <p>Create a safe haven where women in recovery can walk in freedom, and be empowered and equipped to live a life with dignity and purpose.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-warm">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">What we believe</span>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Every woman is uniquely created by God.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
        <p class="lede">She deserves access to the basics. Food. Shelter. Healthcare. A door that locks.</p>
        <p>We believe that a woman leaving a recovery program is not a risk to be managed. She is someone who has already done the hardest thing, and who needs a bridge long enough to reach solid ground. Most programs end at discharge. <strong>The gap that opens up right after is where we work.</strong></p>
        <p>We serve women regardless of their ability to pay, and we do not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, disability, age, or any other characteristic protected by law.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-paper">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Our charitable purpose</span>
        <h2 class="sec-title">What we are organized to do.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
        <p>Oasis Women Center is organized and operated exclusively for charitable and educational purposes within the meaning of Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code.</p>
        <p>The organization provides transitional housing, transportation assistance, educational support, and mental health resources for women who have completed a drug or alcohol recovery program, to promote their successful reintegration into society and to help them achieve stability and independent living.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-warm">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Who we will serve</span>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Who the house is for.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
        <p>Women who have completed a drug and/or alcohol recovery program and who are homeless, at imminent risk of homelessness, or otherwise without safe and stable housing.</p>
        <p>Residents generally are low income or very low income, at or below 30 percent of the Area Median Income as defined by HUD, or can otherwise demonstrate financial hardship. Priority goes to women in immediate danger, women who are literally homeless, and women with the fewest alternatives.</p>
        <p><strong>Selection is never based on ability to pay.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-clay">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Where we will be</span>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Denton County, Texas.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
        <p>We will serve women in the Denton County area.</p>
        <p>Our transitional house will be in a location that will be kept confidential. This is standard practice for residential programs serving women in crisis, as a means of safety and privacy for residents. For anything you need, our phone number and email are on the <a href="/contact/" style="text-decoration:underline">contact page</a>.</p>

